{"connection_id": "BC-6055a4fcb607c935e1a3", "ref": "Barnabas 18:1", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "two ways of light and darkness", "note": "The commentary contrasts a guided way with a path of straying; Barnabas introduces two ways, one of light and one of darkness, extending that moral division in early Christian instruction."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cb3abf5ea3c7826f4ff5", "ref": "Barnabas 20:1", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "dark way and its deeds", "note": "The commentary names a group defined by straying; Barnabas details the way of darkness through its destructive practices, making the wrong path concrete."}
{"connection_id": "BC-98702e43ae0fbb16e80d", "ref": "Didache 1:1", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Way of Life and Way of Death", "note": "The commentary's road splits between a favored and a ruined group; this early Christian teaching opens with two ways, one of life and one of death."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8f3a5e83a42166be596a", "ref": "Epistle of Barnabas 18:1", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "way of light and way of darkness", "note": "The commentary's found path is associated with brightness and its lost path with dark affliction; Barnabas frames its ethical teaching as the two ways of light and darkness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a1eeddb100b31c929104", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.10.1", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "cloud and sea in the exodus", "note": "The commentary begins its wrath scene with Israel rescued from an enemy; Paul recalls that all Israel passed under the cloud and through the sea, reframing the rescue for the church."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7058a2321339ec44f966", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.10.10", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "grumbling and the destroyer", "note": "The commentary connects Israel's rebellion with wrath; Paul warns the congregation not to grumble as the wilderness generation did, whose people were destroyed."}
{"connection_id": "BC-da88cb4ceea8ee0f6925", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.10.11", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Israel's story written for later readers", "note": "The commentary uses Israel's provision and punishment to frame the path of the present reader; Paul explicitly says these events were written as instruction for those upon whom the ends of the ages have come."}
{"connection_id": "BC-719a4a5304fb0e4d566b", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.10.2", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "baptized into Moses", "note": "The commentary recalls Israel's deliverance under Moses; Paul interprets the cloud and sea crossing as a baptism into Moses."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0a470a344f9271b949e5", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.10.3", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "spiritual food in the wilderness", "note": "The commentary names manna as provision; Paul says all Israel ate the same spiritual food, reading the gift typologically."}
{"connection_id": "BC-291c408ef1166a3bdfd1", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.10.3", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "all ate spiritual food", "note": "The commentary's wilderness food is recalled by Paul as the spiritual food shared by the whole exodus generation, a Christian rereading of the manna gift."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e19c5b986e983ff24841", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.10.4", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "spiritual drink from the rock", "note": "The commentary recalls God's provision during the journey; Paul says Israel drank from a spiritual rock and identifies that rock with Christ."}
{"connection_id": "BC-78e64dbaafbfe5854cc5", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.10.5", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "God not pleased in the wilderness", "note": "The commentary makes divine pleasure the opposite of wrath; Paul says God was not pleased with most of the manna-fed generation and their bodies were scattered in the wilderness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9baaa94933fff07e3971", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.10.6", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "wilderness events as examples", "note": "The commentary reads Israel's food and transgression as a warning; Paul tells the church that these events became examples so they would not desire evil as Israel did."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d023c5fca9fb8a0a4736", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.10.7", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "warning from the calf episode", "note": "The commentary uses the calf to connect provision, idolatry, and wrath; Paul invokes the calf tradition to warn his readers not to be idolaters."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8acccc3ffc66c1b10f9f", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.15.43", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "dishonor transformed into glory", "note": "The commentary's two faces foreshadow differing final bodily states; Paul contrasts a body sown in dishonor and weakness with one raised in glory and power."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cf601f395245cd7d0f20", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.4.5", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "praise given when the Lord comes", "note": "The commentary glosses hamd as praise and approval and names the day of recompense; Paul says the Lord's coming reveals hidden motives and each person then receives praise from God."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e7e4d7a893bed33e0698", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.4.5", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "praise from God at final judgment", "note": "The commentary opens its contrast with hamd, praise and approval, and names a day of reckoning; Paul locates each person's praise from God at the Lord's coming judgment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cf065e31432b64e1d5c0", "ref": "SBLGNT:1John.1.6", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "walking in darkness denies fellowship", "note": "The commentary distinguishes a guided way from straying; John says claiming fellowship while walking in darkness is falsehood."}
{"connection_id": "BC-034bb6b36e22f0f5f5f2", "ref": "SBLGNT:1John.1.7", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "walking in light brings cleansing", "note": "The commentary connects the favored way with a visible sign of well-being; John says walking in the light brings fellowship and cleansing from sin."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8e3f88b77912cd407a6c", "ref": "SBLGNT:1John.1.7", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "walking in light, fellowship, and cleansing", "note": "The commentary says the favored path is traveled with companions and ends in forgiveness; John links walking in light to fellowship with one another and cleansing from sin."}
{"connection_id": "BC-69210afe0962e3fa9f5d", "ref": "SBLGNT:1John.1.9", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "confession followed by forgiveness and cleansing", "note": "The commentary's grace side includes forgiveness after wrongdoing; John says confession is met by God's faithful forgiveness and cleansing from unrighteousness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6e7d7f0b8f3c05e67bd2", "ref": "SBLGNT:1John.2.11", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "darkness blinds the eyes and obscures the way", "note": "The commentary's hostile face includes disturbed eyes and its theological result is waylessness; this verse says hatred leaves a person in darkness, blinded in the eyes, ignorant of where to go."}
{"connection_id": "BC-15e25c9f98da34be45d2", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.2.10", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "mercy makes a people", "note": "The commentary says a group receives favor while another strays; Peter says those once not a people have become God's people and those without mercy have received mercy."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f349abf4a3f39da4b250", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.2.10", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "formerly without mercy, now receiving mercy", "note": "The commentary's two groups are distinguished by bestowed ni'ma versus divine disfavor; Peter contrasts being not a people and without mercy with becoming God's people and receiving mercy."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4f628c67f63145db18ed", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.2.9", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "called from darkness to light", "note": "The commentary names the favored as a gathered community on a path; Peter describes a chosen people called out of darkness into God's marvelous light."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a3ec303d75690e19b817", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.2.9", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "chosen people called from darkness to light", "note": "The commentary's favored community is both chosen and visibly luminous; Peter calls a chosen people out of darkness into God's marvelous light."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4f98e60610295b03f3ad", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.3.12", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "the Lord's face against evil", "note": "The commentary says the face carries the sign of wrath or favor; Peter says the Lord's eyes are on the righteous and his face is against those who do evil, applying the contrast to Christian conduct."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e82983ff35ce7a531a29", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.3.12", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the Lord's eyes on righteous and face against evil", "note": "The commentary contrasts a favored group with those under divine disfavor and makes face and eye central; Peter juxtaposes the Lord's eyes upon the righteous with the Lord's face against evildoers."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9d2dc32bb6ade72b4ee8", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.4.14", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "glory's Spirit resting upon the reviled", "note": "The commentary's favor is something that descends upon a group; Peter calls reviled believers blessed because the Spirit of glory and of God rests upon them."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2038edff4257543d7ca7", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Thess.1.10", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rescue from coming wrath", "note": "The commentary juxtaposes wrath with a saving, favorable end; Paul describes Jesus as the one who rescues believers from the wrath that is coming."}

