{"connection_id": "BC-130a8c14b3b34a2926e3", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.10.23", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "blessed eyes that see", "note": "The commentary turns favor into an eye's state; Jesus calls the eyes blessed that see what prophets and kings desired to see."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ad38ffa633e3f1ec19c5", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.13.28", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "prophets inside, others excluded", "note": "The commentary names prophets among the favored and contrasts them with the lost; Jesus describes people seeing Abraham, Isaac, Jacob, and the prophets in God's kingdom while they themselves are cast out."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b07dc5e280fc3de20d82", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.13.29", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "gathered company at the kingdom table", "note": "The commentary describes the favored as companions on a path; Jesus pictures people coming from every direction to recline together in God's kingdom."}
{"connection_id": "BC-55923cf92060a1621c0f", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.15.24", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "lost and found at a father's welcome", "note": "The commentary contrasts being lost with a forgiven return to the way; the father identifies the returned son as formerly lost and now found and celebrates him."}
{"connection_id": "BC-26a6c549791e8520befe", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.15.4", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the shepherd seeks the lost", "note": "The commentary names those who stray and asks for guidance; Jesus depicts a shepherd going after one lost sheep until he finds it, turning the straying image into a search for restoration."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2ef32326164626c0eb15", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.15.7", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "joy when the lost repents", "note": "The commentary says some receive forgiveness rather than wrath; Jesus says heaven rejoices over one sinner who repents, interpreting recovery of the lost as joy rather than rejection."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ca358f323f35e6a5978e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.7.38", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "weeping at Jesus' feet", "note": "The commentary recalls favored people falling in worship and weeping; Luke depicts a woman weeping at Jesus' feet, wetting and wiping them, an embodied act of grief and devotion that Jesus receives in a forgiveness scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-abab646d4cd005d9608f", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.13.43", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "righteous shine at the end", "note": "The commentary contrasts the faces of two groups on the day; Jesus says the righteous will shine like the sun in their Father's kingdom after the wicked are removed."}
{"connection_id": "BC-383aa438d90540ea1c73", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.13.43", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "righteous shining at the consummation", "note": "The commentary's final-day favored faces shine; Jesus says the righteous will shine like the sun in their Father's kingdom."}
{"connection_id": "BC-587f40ce74f57a6a0377", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.17.2", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Jesus' face shining like the sun", "note": "The commentary gives the blessed face a luminous aspect; the transfiguration states that Jesus' face shone like the sun, within a scene of divine approval."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f0c82efa330b4b4a4190", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.21", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "well-done approval and entry into joy", "note": "The commentary glosses hamd as approval or being pleased; the master praises the faithful servant and admits that servant into the master's joy."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fd0c0f919fe8fb4434b9", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.34", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "invitation to the Father's blessed", "note": "The commentary distinguishes those receiving favor from those under wrath at the day; the Son of Man invites those at his right hand as blessed by the Father into the prepared kingdom."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f708f4ea0bc2b7c32d14", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.34", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "blessed inherit the kingdom", "note": "The commentary's favored group receives a divine gift and shares a destination; the king calls the blessed to inherit the kingdom prepared for them."}
{"connection_id": "BC-29cdcbaeae8fadf2e9c5", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.41", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "cursed ones depart from the judge", "note": "The commentary uses a passive label for those upon whom wrath has fallen; here the king explicitly addresses the cursed and sends them away, making the judge's agency overt."}
{"connection_id": "BC-070cebfd3237a3852597", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.41", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "cursed depart to punishment", "note": "The commentary's second group bears divine wrath and abasement; the king's opposite verdict sends the cursed away to the prepared fire."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0d33e270f831b4343948", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.46", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "two final destinies", "note": "The commentary's day carries two opposite outcomes; Matthew closes the judgment scene with punishment for one group and eternal life for the other."}

