{"connection_id": "BC-57ecc8db3b6c2d6f8d7d", "ref": "WLC:1Kgs.18.39", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "faces down and confession after divine fire", "note": "The commentary associates prostration and praise with a divinely favored manifestation; after the fire, the people fall on their faces and confess that YHWH is God."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5df2cae1f20681e0019d", "ref": "WLC:1Sam.1.18", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "favor sought and a no-longer-sad face", "note": "The commentary makes favor visible in a changed face; after Hannah asks to find favor and receives Eli's word of peace, she eats and her face is no longer as before."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4c4d83bb56d5beb90808", "ref": "WLC:1Sam.14.27", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "eyes brighten after food", "note": "The commentary says benefaction cools the eye and recalls food given in the wilderness; Jonathan eats honey and his eyes brighten, a concrete bodily sign of refreshment after nourishment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b3903878c9eae250bfd5", "ref": "WLC:2Chr.7.3", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "faces to the ground in praise", "note": "The commentary depicts the favored, guided people prostrating and praises God; Israel sees divine glory and fire, bows with faces to the ground, worships, and gives thanks."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b6964ddff9eaaccd2416", "ref": "WLC:Amos.5.18", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "expected day reversed as darkness", "note": "The commentary says the day carries both wrath and deliverance; Amos warns those expecting the Lord's day that it will be darkness rather than light, a direct reversal of presumed favor."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f1f98e42baacf6aecb9a", "ref": "WLC:Dan.12.2", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "two outcomes at awakening", "note": "The commentary places two groups under the judgment of the day; Daniel describes some awakening to everlasting life and others to shame and everlasting contempt."}
{"connection_id": "BC-dd771408be93609232b4", "ref": "WLC:Dan.12.2", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "end-time life or contempt", "note": "The commentary describes the final day as two visibly different destinies; Daniel's resurrection vision divides the sleepers between everlasting life and everlasting contempt."}
{"connection_id": "BC-dde57ca3e661508d3a02", "ref": "WLC:Dan.12.3", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "righteous shine like stars", "note": "The commentary says the day's outcome appears on faces; Daniel says the wise and those who lead many to righteousness will shine like the brightness of the sky and stars."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7416affba45cec998437", "ref": "WLC:Dan.12.3", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the righteous shine", "note": "The commentary's favored faces are luminous; Daniel says the wise, including those who turn many to righteousness, will shine like the expanse and stars."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8e6bb43c37ee15385170", "ref": "WLC:Dan.5.6", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "judgment changing a king's facial color", "note": "The commentary reads judgment in the body's face; after the divine writing appears, Belshazzar's facial color changes and his body is visibly shaken."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9f5ca7287cf15bebf070", "ref": "WLC:Dan.7.10", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "court sits and books open", "note": "The commentary names a day of recompense for two groups; Daniel's vision shows the court seated and the books opened before the beast is judged."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fde427141633c34c366a", "ref": "WLC:Dan.9.7", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "shame of face under sin and judgment", "note": "The commentary's adverse group bears a humiliated face; Daniel assigns shame of face to Israel and its rulers because of unfaithfulness before God's righteousness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2db86ab80fdbb71351cf", "ref": "WLC:Deut.10.10", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "God does not destroy after Moses' intercession", "note": "The commentary's wrath sequence is countered by forgiveness and a continued path; Deuteronomy's retrospective account says YHWH listened to Moses and was unwilling to destroy Israel after the calf."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2470146837d02fe78326", "ref": "WLC:Deut.10.12", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "walking all God's ways after the calf intercession", "note": "The commentary's mercy sequence ends in a rightly lived way; after the calf-and-intercession complex, Moses states the requirement to fear, love, serve, and walk in all God's ways."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6e94353c60e7ac138b0e", "ref": "WLC:Deut.11.28", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "curse for turning aside from the way", "note": "The commentary places divine disfavor upon those who lose the way; Deuteronomy defines the curse through disobedience and turning aside from the commanded way."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f7ca31f89e16ceab09bc", "ref": "WLC:Deut.30.15", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "life and death set before Israel", "note": "The commentary distinguishes two groups by the path they take; Moses sets life and good against death and evil before Israel, framing the division as a consequential choice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ff26d9b66b5568f0031a", "ref": "WLC:Deut.30.19", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "choose life for descendants", "note": "The commentary presents a path shared by those receiving favor; Moses calls heaven and earth as witnesses and urges Israel to choose life, including life for their descendants."}
{"connection_id": "BC-414add84ce1a04ed8097", "ref": "WLC:Deut.30.19", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "life and good against death and evil", "note": "The commentary divides recipients into contrary divine outcomes; Moses sets life and death, blessing and curse before Israel and urges the choice of life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5688f5814f46bd35714d", "ref": "WLC:Deut.30.20", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "love, clinging, and long life", "note": "The commentary describes the favored as walking a path together; Moses urges Israel to love God, cling to him, and live, tying fidelity to a continuing way of life."}

