{"connection_id": "BC-c2075fa3bb712b0cd77b", "ref": "WLC:Deut.5.21", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Hebrew חמד as coveting", "note": "The source commentary labels Arabic ح م د and glosses hamd as approval; Deuteronomy uses the related Hebrew form for prohibited desire of a neighbor's property, a contrary ethical use that cautions against collapsing the senses."}
{"connection_id": "BC-40aeee3c4a2d3be7006f", "ref": "WLC:Deut.8.16", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "manna, testing, good outcome", "note": "The commentary recalls provision followed by a warning against transgression; Moses says manna humbled and tested Israel, with God intending good for them at the end."}
{"connection_id": "BC-afba4818b6c9148e311c", "ref": "WLC:Deut.8.16", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "manna given for eventual good", "note": "The commentary combines wilderness provision with moral testing and a beneficial end; Deuteronomy says YHWH fed Israel manna to humble and test them, in order to do them good in the end."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b182e272f23c57816107", "ref": "WLC:Deut.8.3", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "manna teaches dependence", "note": "The commentary presents food as a gift that can be followed by judgment; Moses says hunger and manna taught Israel that life depends on God's word, giving the provision a lesson beyond nourishment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-aeafb7f5d90dcf4b05e0", "ref": "WLC:Deut.8.3", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "manna given in hunger", "note": "The commentary treats manna as divine provision joined to moral direction; Deuteronomy says God fed Israel manna in order to teach dependence on the divine word."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e4eb857597fdb266485c", "ref": "WLC:Deut.9.19", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Moses pleads during wrath", "note": "The commentary presents Moses returning from the calf in anger and grief; Deuteronomy says he feared the Lord's anger and recounts that the Lord listened to his plea."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c7d6434471ac39a4c99f", "ref": "WLC:Deut.9.19", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Moses fears anger and wrath", "note": "The commentary's calf scene holds wrath and a way back together; Moses says he feared the anger and wrath that would destroy Israel, yet YHWH listened to him."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0322e0173fa81f5be882", "ref": "WLC:Deut.9.20", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Aaron and intercession", "note": "The commentary connects the calf with threatened wrath; Deuteronomy says the Lord was angry enough with Aaron to destroy him and that Moses prayed for him too."}
{"connection_id": "BC-355454c4d6ac437f323d", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.12.14", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "deeds brought to judgment", "note": "The commentary makes the day the time when the two outcomes are borne; Ecclesiastes says God will bring every deed, including what is hidden, into judgment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fd44a225ccf334c14563", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.8.1", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wisdom changes a stern face", "note": "The commentary describes anger as a scowling face and favor as softness; Ecclesiastes says wisdom brightens a person's face and changes its severity."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a85a6aab5db340d1a326", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.8.1", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wisdom lighting and changing a hard face", "note": "The commentary says favor makes the face soft rather than frowning; Ecclesiastes says wisdom lights a person's face and changes its hardness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5e642a35dd127c9f07e1", "ref": "WLC:Exod.15.13", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "rescued people led by God", "note": "The commentary places manna and quail after Israel's rescue from an enemy; this song says YHWH redeemed and led that people, supplying the narrative transition into the wilderness provision."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c93de8b85964aa57d862", "ref": "WLC:Exod.16.13", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "manna and quail provision", "note": "The commentary says God reminds Israel of manna and quail; Exodus describes quail at evening and manna with the morning dew."}
{"connection_id": "BC-01a0ebd82cf3741b021f", "ref": "WLC:Exod.16.13", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "quail at evening", "note": "The commentary names quail among Israel's bestowed food; Exodus reports quail covering the camp in the evening, the same wilderness provision scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-43c9e77ba2d17e66ea0a", "ref": "WLC:Exod.16.15", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "manna explicitly called YHWH's bread-gift", "note": "The commentary's descent of ni'ma after rescue is named here by the Israelites as bread that YHWH has given them to eat; it supplies the explicit attribution absent from the passive wrath phrase."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0d85cd03b599e6e73a8d", "ref": "WLC:Exod.16.31", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "manna's honey-like taste", "note": "The commentary develops ni'ma as softness, delicacy, and a restful sensory condition; Exodus describes manna as white and tasting like wafers made with honey."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e9917d4f5c065bfdf69a", "ref": "WLC:Exod.16.4", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "bread from heaven as a test", "note": "The commentary recalls manna before warning Israel not to transgress over provision; Exodus says the bread from heaven also tests whether they will walk in God's instruction."}
{"connection_id": "BC-aa01d6a7ef7b9b4c0e8c", "ref": "WLC:Exod.16.4", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "bread rained from heaven", "note": "The commentary's gift that comes down upon the rescued Israelites meets YHWH's promise to rain bread from heaven for them; the verse makes the descent and divine giver explicit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-05682d4279e1305c311e", "ref": "WLC:Exod.32.10", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "divine anger at the calf", "note": "The commentary links the calf episode with wrath upon the people; God tells Moses that his anger burns against Israel and threatens their destruction."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2e74960a94e9f47aec7b", "ref": "WLC:Exod.32.10", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "YHWH's burning anger at the calf", "note": "The commentary says calf worship incurs wrath from the Lord; YHWH tells Moses that his anger burns and proposes consuming the calf-making people."}
{"connection_id": "BC-410b48c8305339ac5a7d", "ref": "WLC:Exod.32.11", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "wrath against the people brought from Egypt", "note": "The commentary recalls a people rescued from an enemy who then risk wrath; Moses explicitly asks why YHWH's anger burns against the people brought out of Egypt."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c26393e59cfc45f01bc4", "ref": "WLC:Exod.32.14", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "anger relented after intercession", "note": "The commentary places wrath beside forgiveness; after Moses pleads for Israel, Exodus says the Lord relented from the threatened disaster."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9871ba15b8e06df7261b", "ref": "WLC:Exod.32.14", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "YHWH relents from disaster", "note": "The commentary juxtaposes descending wrath with abundant forgiveness; the calf narrative itself reports that YHWH relented from the disaster announced for the people."}
{"connection_id": "BC-56ca5a548b196c5072e6", "ref": "WLC:Exod.32.19", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Moses at the calf", "note": "The commentary recalls Moses returning angry after the calf; Exodus depicts him seeing the calf and breaking the tablets in anger at the foot of the mountain."}
{"connection_id": "BC-68a3450d4c2ac795c1e7", "ref": "WLC:Exod.32.19", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Moses angry on seeing the calf", "note": "The commentary says Moses returned to his people angry and grieving after the calf; Exodus narrates his anger blazing when he saw the calf and dancing."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1a171b7f781c277a51a5", "ref": "WLC:Exod.32.32", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Moses offers himself for the people's sin", "note": "The commentary describes wrath as falling on the culpable group; Moses instead offers to be blotted from God's book if Israel cannot be forgiven, a different placement of the consequence within the same calf scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-55a0e1880a9d096aee9e", "ref": "WLC:Exod.32.35", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "plague after the calf", "note": "The commentary's calf-makers receive divine anger and abasement; Exodus closes the narrative with YHWH striking the people with a plague because of the calf."}

item.stdout:
{"connection_id": "BC-5bc6f22700dc66f00fbf", "ref": "WLC:Exod.32.8", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "calf and turning aside from the way", "note": "The commentary makes the calf episode a junction of wrath and losing the way; Exodus says Israel quickly turned aside from the commanded way and made a calf."}
{"connection_id": "BC-507eb248b25c9b9ae647", "ref": "WLC:Exod.33.17", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "finding favor in God's eyes", "note": "The commentary glosses hamd through approval and turns ni'ma into favor bestowed on people; YHWH tells Moses that he has found favor in his eyes and is known by name."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bc39cedd1dcda725512a", "ref": "WLC:Exod.34.29", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Moses' face shines after the calf", "note": "The commentary recalls Moses' anger at the calf and contrasts two faces; after the renewed covenant, Exodus says Moses' face shines from speaking with God."}
{"connection_id": "BC-85b6d6753b756564f4e8", "ref": "WLC:Exod.34.29", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Moses' radiant face", "note": "The commentary makes favor legible in a brightened face; Moses' face radiates after speaking with YHWH, supplying a concrete biblical face-of-divine-encounter image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-883033633976be9d8c7e", "ref": "WLC:Exod.34.6", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "merciful and slow to anger", "note": "The commentary sets wrath against favor; after the calf episode God proclaims himself compassionate and slow to anger, framing the same episode with mercy."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6a7b591d72435e5dace2", "ref": "WLC:Exod.34.6", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "merciful and gracious, slow to anger", "note": "The commentary opposes rida and ghadaab while retaining forgiveness after the calf; YHWH's self-proclamation names mercy, grace, patience, and steadfast love in that same aftermath."}
{"connection_id": "BC-61bc7fc72af52ce25f5d", "ref": "WLC:Exod.34.7", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "forgiving and judging wrongdoing", "note": "The commentary holds forgiveness beside the wrath that falls on others; the covenant proclamation says God forgives iniquity while also addressing wrongdoing, refusing to collapse mercy into impunity."}
{"connection_id": "BC-eec3d7009a11277b2f08", "ref": "WLC:Exod.34.7", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "forgiveness and unremitted guilt", "note": "The commentary holds mercy beside real wrath rather than treating them as interchangeable; the continuation both forgives iniquity and says guilt is not simply cleared."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4a45d680a313960260cf", "ref": "WLC:Exod.34.8", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Moses bows in response to mercy", "note": "The commentary joins the favored, guided community with prostration; directly after the mercy-and-forgiveness proclamation Moses hurriedly bows his head to the earth and worships."}
{"connection_id": "BC-730d7d74f4521baafb5f", "ref": "WLC:Exod.34.9", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "favor, pardon, and inheritance", "note": "The commentary's favorable group receives grace and travels together; Moses asks that, if he has found favor, God pardon the people and take them as an inheritance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-99913f2cbca27bde7258", "ref": "WLC:Ezek.18.23", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "turn from a way and live", "note": "The commentary says the forgiven turn toward the right way; God asks whether he delights in the wicked person's death rather than in that person's turning from the way and living."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3085270d9f28b0b6915e", "ref": "WLC:Ezek.34.16", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the shepherd seeks the lost", "note": "The commentary names those who stray and asks for guidance; Ezekiel's shepherd promises to seek the lost, bring back the straying, bind the injured, and strengthen the weak."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4c6af886913e07714d41", "ref": "WLC:Ezek.7.18", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "shame on every face in judgment", "note": "The commentary's final adverse side bears a darkened or humiliated face; Ezekiel's judgment scene says shame will be on every face as people put on sackcloth and tremble."}
{"connection_id": "BC-77b4288b4581b46a85b7", "ref": "WLC:Ezra.10.1", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "weeping and face-down confession", "note": "The commentary's guided, forgiven figures prostrate themselves; Ezra's confession is made while weeping and lying face down before God's house, gathering a repentant community."}
{"connection_id": "BC-39fcd38e69df16b4b897", "ref": "WLC:Ezra.9.6", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "shame prevents lifting the face", "note": "The commentary's adverse outcome is a humiliated face under sin; Ezra confesses being ashamed and unable to lift his face to God because Israel's iniquities have risen high."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9cc14788578d8696277e", "ref": "WLC:Ezra.9.8", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "favor that brightens eyes", "note": "The commentary says favor softens and cools the eye; Ezra describes a brief gift of divine favor that brightens the community's eyes and revives them in captivity."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9eba16c5b7adef8443ff", "ref": "WLC:Ezra.9.8", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "favor brightening the eyes", "note": "The commentary gives favor a softened face and cooled eye; Ezra calls the remnant's reprieve divine favor and says it brightens their eyes and gives reviving."}
{"connection_id": "BC-104605023f12a12c9825", "ref": "WLC:Gen.17.3", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "falling on the face before covenant blessing", "note": "The commentary's chosen and blessed figures fall in prostration; Abram falls on his face immediately before God gives covenantal promises of fruitfulness and nations."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cbcacdafab59d344d03d", "ref": "WLC:Gen.3.6", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Hebrew חמד as desirable", "note": "The source commentary labels Arabic ح م د and glosses hamd as praise or approval; Genesis uses the related Hebrew form for the tree as desirable to the eyes, showing a desire or attractiveness sense rather than the same act of praising."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9bbd61acc30b64cb41e9", "ref": "WLC:Gen.3.6", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Hebrew nehmad as desirable", "note": "The commentary glosses hamd as approving or finding something good; Hebrew h-m-d is plausibly cognate but in this verse calls the forbidden tree desirable to the eyes, so the semantic divergence rather than a direct parallel is the point."}
{"connection_id": "BC-068ebf46fa70771b0442", "ref": "WLC:Gen.32.11", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Jacob fears Esau", "note": "The commentary contrasts wrath with favor borne on a face; Jacob asks God to rescue him because he fears Esau, setting up the reversal in their meeting."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a219de71b68539da606b", "ref": "WLC:Gen.33.10", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Esau's face as God's face", "note": "The commentary says favor is visible in the face; Jacob says Esau's favorable reception is like seeing God's face, making acceptance itself the face's mark."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8def1f1aaf375ba7a24b", "ref": "WLC:Gen.33.4", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Esau welcomes Jacob", "note": "The commentary sets a wrathful face against a softened one; Esau runs to Jacob, embraces and kisses him, then they weep, turning anticipated hostility into welcome."}
{"connection_id": "BC-14808e2211bf9042a005", "ref": "WLC:Gen.4.5", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "anger and a fallen face", "note": "The commentary says anger leaves a visible face; after Cain's offering is not regarded, he becomes very angry and his face falls."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3bd3cd78e0501359b6b4", "ref": "WLC:Gen.4.6", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "anger and fallen face", "note": "The commentary connects anger with a scowling face; God asks Cain why he is angry and why his face has fallen, an unusually close bodily scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bb7afda32a69dad4826a", "ref": "WLC:Gen.4.7", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "anger at the door and choice", "note": "The commentary makes the angry face part of a moral division; God tells Cain that sin crouches at the door and that he must rule over it, turning the fallen-face scene toward a choice of conduct."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9e80f9e41ff650e98cb9", "ref": "WLC:Gen.4.7", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "lifted countenance versus sin at the door", "note": "The commentary's angry face is the inverse of divine approval; God's reply to Cain contrasts the lifting granted for doing well with sin crouching at the door if he does not."}
{"connection_id": "BC-90056789fa700afc7e38", "ref": "WLC:Hab.3.2", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "mercy remembered in wrath", "note": "The commentary says God's days include both punishment and pardon; Habakkuk asks God to remember mercy in the midst of wrath."}
{"connection_id": "BC-47a33ae2d71e051f5897", "ref": "WLC:Hos.11.8", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God's compassion turns within him", "note": "The commentary contrasts wrath with the forgiveness of others; God asks how he can give Ephraim up and says his compassion is kindled within him."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a049d061ddf46d3e066f", "ref": "WLC:Hos.11.9", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "anger withheld from Ephraim", "note": "The commentary says wrath falls on some while others are spared; God says he will not execute fierce anger or destroy Ephraim, because he is God and not man."}

