{"connection_id": "BC-4fed8b90734003f27088", "ref": "WLC:Joel.2.13", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "return, mercy, and relenting from disaster", "note": "The commentary's forgiving end asks for turning back after wrongdoing; Joel calls for a return of heart because God is gracious, merciful, slow to anger, and relents from calamity."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d47aab182768ff23af8f", "ref": "WLC:Joel.2.6", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "faces growing pale before the divine day", "note": "The commentary describes an anger-marked red face and a final day; Joel's approaching day makes the nations writhe and all faces gather pallor, a related but distinct facial response."}
{"connection_id": "BC-db4b5f1fd84d8ecf550a", "ref": "WLC:Jonah.3.10", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "judgment averted after repentance", "note": "The commentary says the day may bring wrath or forgiveness; after Nineveh turns from evil, God sees its deeds and does not bring the announced disaster."}
{"connection_id": "BC-54e9111b933a23fdc4f9", "ref": "WLC:Jonah.4.11", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "compassion for Nineveh's inhabitants", "note": "The commentary says others are forgiven rather than struck by wrath; God answers Jonah by naming the many people in Nineveh for whom he has compassion."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bfc700e78c1c14d0d676", "ref": "WLC:Jonah.4.2", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "slow anger toward a spared city", "note": "The commentary says some receive wrath while others are forgiven; Jonah objects that God is gracious and slow to anger after Nineveh is spared, making mercy to an outside city the point of his complaint."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d172e3447ae3356e86cd", "ref": "WLC:Jonah.4.9", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Jonah's anger at a spared plant", "note": "The commentary connects anger with a bodily state and with whether wrath falls; Jonah becomes angry over the plant, while God uses that response to challenge his anger at Nineveh's rescue."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ced2c321af15804faa31", "ref": "WLC:Lam.2.10", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "dust on heads and faces lowered", "note": "The commentary has dust descend over adverse faces; Jerusalem's mourners put dust on their heads and lower their heads to the ground under judgment, a concrete adjacent image of abasement."}
{"connection_id": "BC-82390ce66292c15f9e41", "ref": "WLC:Lam.3.31", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the Lord does not reject forever", "note": "The commentary pairs wrath and forgiveness; Lamentations says the Lord does not cast off forever, even after bringing grief."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0e7a6e9bab216b2d3c1a", "ref": "WLC:Lam.3.32", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "compassion after grief", "note": "The commentary says punishment and pardon are both among God's days; Lamentations says God will show compassion according to abundant steadfast love after causing grief."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4ed9e9ba6ad39725ca35", "ref": "WLC:Lam.3.33", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God does not afflict from his heart", "note": "The commentary distinguishes wrath from bestowed favor; Lamentations says God does not willingly afflict or grieve human beings."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d84b65dd677ed7f5d4ec", "ref": "WLC:Lam.4.8", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "faces blackened by disaster", "note": "The commentary contrasts luminous favored faces with a darkened other side; Lamentations says the sufferers' faces are blacker than soot."}
{"connection_id": "BC-92bafb216def563ad4da", "ref": "WLC:Lev.1.4", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Hebrew ratsah and accepted atonement", "note": "The commentary calls rida the opposite of anger; Hebrew ratsah, a likely cognate of Arabic rida, says the offering will be accepted for the worshiper to make atonement."}
{"connection_id": "BC-15033b8c6664cc54f819", "ref": "WLC:Lev.26.17", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God's face set against the disobedient", "note": "The commentary pairs adverse divine judgment with face imagery; YHWH says he will set his face against Israel, after which they are defeated and flee."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2e230661f369229c5b1f", "ref": "WLC:Mal.2.6", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "walking with God and turning many from iniquity", "note": "The commentary's gifted people travel a way together and turn toward guidance; Levi is said to have walked with God in peace and uprightness and to have turned many from iniquity."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7039cd793908f337237d", "ref": "WLC:Mal.3.18", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "distinction between righteous and wicked", "note": "The commentary divides the guided from those under wrath; Malachi says the day will make the distinction between the righteous who serve God and the wicked who do not."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5599ddffbdd5e0a8ca4b", "ref": "WLC:Mal.3.19", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "day as burning judgment", "note": "The commentary calls the day the bearer of wrath and recompense; Malachi describes the coming day as an oven that consumes the arrogant and evildoers."}
{"connection_id": "BC-acbcb7629065b0e21c11", "ref": "WLC:Mal.3.20", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "healing sun for those who fear God", "note": "The commentary contrasts a wrath-marked face with a softened, favored one; Malachi sets the coming day against a sun of righteousness with healing for those who fear God."}
{"connection_id": "BC-df687759f97beee98412", "ref": "WLC:Mic.7.18", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God does not retain anger", "note": "The commentary puts forgiveness beside wrath; Micah praises a God who does not hold anger forever and delights in steadfast love."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e5f7f577fa61fcfc15a1", "ref": "WLC:Mic.7.18", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God does not retain anger", "note": "The commentary sets divine anger against rida or merciful approval; Micah says God does not retain anger forever because he delights in steadfast love."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5237121bca3cdda3b8f0", "ref": "WLC:Mic.7.19", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "iniquity cast away", "note": "The commentary says some receive forgiveness instead of wrath; Micah says God will again show compassion and cast the people's sins into the depths of the sea."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6592fafa307549acae11", "ref": "WLC:Neh.8.6", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "communal bowing and praise", "note": "The commentary's selected group falls in prostration; at Ezra's blessing the people answer Amen, lift hands, and bow with faces to the ground."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0a3cfe37681c86e5af21", "ref": "WLC:Neh.9.15", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "bread, water, and promised land", "note": "The commentary recalls Israel's rescue and wilderness provision before warning; Nehemiah's prayer recounts bread from heaven, water from rock, and the command to possess the land."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f3f366f932ad9abe8e8d", "ref": "WLC:Neh.9.17", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "slow anger after wilderness rebellion", "note": "The commentary sets the wilderness provision beside wrath and pardon; Nehemiah's communal confession recalls Israel's rebellion and God's readiness to forgive, slow anger, and steadfast love."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b7af35574bc211056fa9", "ref": "WLC:Neh.9.17", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "forgiving God, slow to anger", "note": "The commentary contrasts divine wrath with a forgiving end for those who return; Nehemiah interprets Israel's rebellion through God's readiness to forgive, grace, mercy, and slowness to anger."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ce1b7d255f47916b03ff", "ref": "WLC:Neh.9.18", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "calf called Israel's god", "note": "The commentary names the calf as the concrete offense beneath the wrath; Nehemiah's liturgical retelling specifies Israel's making of the calf and its attribution of the exodus to it."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ecbd7d5e8cba6ef4c9c4", "ref": "WLC:Neh.9.19", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "cloud and fire leading the people", "note": "The commentary's favorable alternative to wrath is a guided road; Nehemiah's retelling says God's mercies did not forsake Israel and that cloud and fire continued to lead them on the way."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b54783cdc884c0d6a4e6", "ref": "WLC:Neh.9.20", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "spirit guides while manna continues", "note": "The commentary joins bestowed provision to a guided way; Nehemiah says God gave his good spirit to instruct Israel and did not withhold manna or water."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ba270891de88e3504fd2", "ref": "WLC:Neh.9.20", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Spirit's instruction and manna", "note": "The commentary couples food, forgiveness, and finding the way; Nehemiah's retrospective prayer says God gave a good spirit to instruct Israel and did not withhold manna or water."}

