{"connection_id": "BC-cc8c0b4606f04abe3114", "ref": "WLC:Isa.42.16", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "blind led from darkness into a way", "note": "The commentary's opposing outcomes are found or lost way; YHWH promises to lead blind people by an unknown way and make darkness light before them."}
{"connection_id": "BC-de573c7a62da142807ac", "ref": "WLC:Isa.48.17", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God teaches and leads", "note": "The commentary treats the favored path as guidance from God; Isaiah names God as the one who teaches what benefits his people and leads them in the way they should go."}
{"connection_id": "BC-28dcd5cc16582ab76dd6", "ref": "WLC:Isa.48.17", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God teaching and leading in the way", "note": "The commentary describes hidaya as the counterpart to falling under wrath; YHWH identifies himself as the one who teaches for profit and leads in the way to go."}
{"connection_id": "BC-24e80bd0aa0f12146db5", "ref": "WLC:Isa.53.6", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "all stray, iniquity laid on one", "note": "The commentary says a straying collective bears wrath upon itself; Isaiah describes all as going astray yet makes YHWH lay the iniquity of all upon a single servant, reversing the placement of consequence."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a32d744aea49db92ea77", "ref": "WLC:Isa.54.8", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "anger-hidden face and lasting compassion", "note": "The commentary contrasts wrath with favor and reads both on the face; Isaiah describes a moment of anger in which God hid his face, set against enduring compassion."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1db197fde98d6a0a3bc7", "ref": "WLC:Isa.54.8", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "face hidden in wrath, everlasting compassion after it", "note": "The commentary treats anger as a visible, descending judgment and favor as its contrary; Isaiah says God hid his face in overflowing wrath for a moment but has everlasting compassion."}
{"connection_id": "BC-aa62d74bce46d3623956", "ref": "WLC:Isa.55.7", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "leave the wicked way and receive pardon", "note": "The commentary contrasts those on the guided path with those who stray and notes that others are forgiven; Isaiah tells the wicked to forsake their way and return to God, who pardons abundantly."}
{"connection_id": "BC-45bde22bcf084b64c3c2", "ref": "WLC:Isa.55.7", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "leave a wicked way and receive pardon", "note": "The commentary gives forgiveness a turn toward guidance; Isaiah calls the wicked to leave their way and return to YHWH, who will have compassion and abundantly pardon."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ab6b1628afb15d3461a2", "ref": "WLC:Isa.57.17", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "anger and a person following his own way", "note": "The commentary links anger to straying from the path; Isaiah says God was angry at iniquity, yet the person went on in the way of his own heart."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a5ff2a0de9a4d3c8e987", "ref": "WLC:Isa.57.18", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "healing and guidance after waywardness", "note": "The commentary's alternative to wrath is forgiveness followed by finding the way; immediately after the angry-wandering image, God says he will heal, lead, and restore comfort."}
{"connection_id": "BC-399c935157307c54f670", "ref": "WLC:Isa.60.5", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "seeing and becoming radiant", "note": "The commentary's favorable face becomes visibly bright; Isaiah says Zion will see and be radiant in the context of restored divine abundance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b73e785664fcc6b35eec", "ref": "WLC:Isa.61.3", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "praise replacing a faint spirit and ashes", "note": "The commentary makes hamd the opposite of blame and favors a softened countenance; Isaiah's restoration gives a garland instead of ashes and a garment of praise instead of a faint spirit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5579a0fc0de6f0a2e668", "ref": "WLC:Isa.65.13", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "servants fed and rejoicing while others hunger and are ashamed", "note": "The commentary's two groups receive contrary bodily conditions; Isaiah contrasts God's servants eating, drinking, and rejoicing with others' hunger, thirst, and shame."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3cb75aa324ed30e6282e", "ref": "WLC:Isa.65.14", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "joyful heart versus pain and broken spirit", "note": "The commentary's favorable face is softened while the adverse face is afflicted; Isaiah continues the division with joyful song from the heart versus cry of pain and a broken spirit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c2de70db94ac307bde52", "ref": "WLC:Isa.66.14", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rejoicing heart and flourishing body versus indignation", "note": "The commentary makes the two divine outcomes bodily visible; Isaiah promises servants a rejoicing heart and flourishing bones while God's indignation is known against enemies."}
{"connection_id": "BC-28d07c402b832582ade0", "ref": "WLC:Isa.66.5", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "joy for the faithful and shame for opponents", "note": "The commentary separates a favored community from an abased one; Isaiah tells those who tremble at God's word that God will appear to their joy while their hostile brethren are put to shame."}
{"connection_id": "BC-14128212b9799d52daa3", "ref": "WLC:Jer.3.12", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "return and no perpetual anger", "note": "The commentary places repentance and forgiveness against wrath; the summons to faithless Israel to return grounds mercy in God's refusal to keep anger forever."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3157b1d9a19bc6e6e8bb", "ref": "WLC:Jer.31.9", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "guided on a straight path without stumbling", "note": "The commentary's favorable group is a company on a road; Jeremiah's restored people are led by streams on a straight path where they do not stumble."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c893c4ea119a6afc9562", "ref": "WLC:Jer.6.16", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the old path refused", "note": "The commentary distinguishes a guided path from straying; Jeremiah asks the people to seek the good way and walk in it for rest, but they answer that they will not walk there."}

