{"connection_id": "BC-6b183972d0e457e95abf", "ref": "WLC:Zeph.3.8", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wrath poured upon a collective", "note": "The commentary dwells on wrath descending upon alayhim; Zephaniah has YHWH say he will pour his indignation and burning anger upon them, making the agent and collective onset explicit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-782fa8207785d1bb1437", "ref": "WLC:Zeph.3.8", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Hebrew alehem under poured anger", "note": "The commentary's grammar turns Arabic alayhim into the site of descending wrath; Hebrew alehem is its cognate prepositional form in Zephaniah's statement that indignation is poured upon them."}
{"connection_id": "BC-30c50300d94ee39f98e1", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 16:20", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "heavenly bread for Israel", "note": "The commentary recalls manna as provision from God; Wisdom calls it food of angels and bread from heaven, giving the wilderness gift a Jewish sapiential interpretation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2028fb053888ff8f60d5", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 16:20", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "heavenly manna suited to every taste", "note": "The commentary draws the ni'ma family's softness and delight out of the manna scene; Wisdom retells that manna as heavenly food containing every pleasure and fitted to each taste."}
{"connection_id": "BC-778fe890834ad291089f", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 16:21", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the gift shows divine sweetness", "note": "The commentary contrasts harsh wrath with the softening quality of favor; Wisdom says the manna displayed God's sweetness toward the children he loved."}
{"connection_id": "BC-75fe6279d6323ddf4109", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 16:26", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "manna teaches God's word sustains", "note": "The commentary presents provision as both gift and test; Wisdom says the manna taught Israel that crops do not sustain people by themselves, but God's word preserves those who trust him."}
{"connection_id": "BC-59f2085ab006f35ef4bd", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 3:9", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "grace and mercy upon the elect", "note": "The commentary's en'amte alayhim makes grace a gift resting upon a chosen group; Wisdom says grace and mercy are upon God's holy ones and care attends the elect."}
{"connection_id": "BC-257bc8824696cb8fe50e", "ref": "Yoma 75a", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "manna distributed by moral standing", "note": "The commentary divides recipients of divine provision from those exposed to wrath; this rabbinic manna tradition differentiates where the righteous, intermediate, and wicked find the food, making provision a visible moral index."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9e6190f1dda35c7a5aaf", "ref": "b. Berakhot 7a", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "mercy restrains anger", "note": "The commentary contrasts divine favor with wrath; this Talmudic prayer asks that God's mercy prevail over his anger, giving the contrast a Jewish devotional formulation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bd7f5e42236460ee7ce7", "ref": "b. Rosh Hashanah 17b", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Moses' mercy liturgy after sin", "note": "The commentary sets wrath and forgiveness together in the calf story; this rabbinic reading of God's revelation to Moses makes reciting the mercy attributes a liturgical route to forgiveness after Israel sins."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9407822feb550dfb08ef", "ref": "b. Shabbat 105b", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "anger enacted through destructive acts", "note": "The commentary describes anger as an inward heat with outward bodily signs; the Talmud warns against tearing clothes, breaking vessels, or scattering money in anger, treating those acts as a dangerous loss of moral control."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8f659282c068c058922d", "ref": "b. Shabbat 88a", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "crowns removed after the calf", "note": "The commentary recalls a bestowed gift followed by wrath after the calf; this rabbinic account says Israel's crowns from Sinai were removed after the calf, interpreting rebellion as loss of received honor."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b3ae95a876c50ac0a39e", "ref": "b. Yoma 85b", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "neighbor's grievance before atonement", "note": "The commentary juxtaposes wrath, pardon, and the way one should follow; this teaching says atonement for wrongs against another person requires first appeasing the injured neighbor."}

